al psychological intervention for migraine\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Biofeedback is a well-established biobehavioral technique that enables patients to gain conscious control over physiological processes such as muscle tension and skin temperature. This is achieved through real-time monitoring devices that provide feedback on bodily states, allowing individuals to learn how to reduce systemic arousal associated with migraine triggers. Research has demonstrated that biofeedback significantly reduces migraine frequency and improves symptom management, making it a valuable non-drug treatment option. Its strong connection to both behavior therapy and cognitive behavioral therapy underscores its integration into broader therapeutic frameworks [Data: Entities (265), Relationships (420, 418, +more)].\",\n            \"summary\": \"Biofeedback is an effective non-pharmacological tool for migraine prevention\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction (MBSR), or mindfulness-based therapy, is a structured program that teaches patients to focus on present-moment awareness and non-judgmental acceptance of sensations and thoughts. Recent randomized studies have shown that MBSR reduces migraine-related disability and improves overall quality of life, positioning it as a recommended adjunctive treatment. The evidence from a randomized study supports its clinical efficacy, and its inclusion in the community reflects growing recognition of mind-body approaches in chronic pain management [Data: Entities (266), Relationships (422, 423, +more)].\",\n            \"summary\": \"Mindfulness-based stress reduction shows clinical benefits for migraine patients\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Meta-analyses play a critical role in synthesizing evidence from multiple clinical studies to assess the overall effectiveness of psychological interventions for migraine. The cited meta-analysis prov